+
+## Checksum-Compatible Resume Projection Integrity
+
+Status: target contract. A run whose root `workflow_checksum` still matches can carry persisted identities that the current program no longer owns. Resume audits every scope it will execute against the current projection and fails closed before any state mutation or effect other than the failure-envelope writes of the classes below. The audit reads only the bundle and state it is given; it loads no workflow, computes no checksum and reads no file, and it never mutates the audited state. The audit takes the raw persisted JSON mapping, never `RunState.to_dict()`.
+
+### Audit points and ordering
+- After schema and root-checksum acceptance, `orchestrator resume` audits the persisted root scope against the entry workflow projection before observability overrides, executor session or process metadata, executor construction and the execution prologue. Failure exits 1, prints `Error: <message>` to standard error and persists only the failure envelope.
+- A `WorkflowExecutor` resuming as the structural root (not a child call-frame executor) rechecks the checksum (`StateManager.calculate_checksum`) and then audits the root scope immediately before its prologue. A child executor does not repeat the root guard.
+- A reached call revalidates frame shape, caller boundary, persisted import alias and lineage. It calls `WorkflowStateProjection.resolve_call_boundary(slot_index, call_step_id)` once per reached call identity; exactly one current boundary must own it and its current import alias selects the callee bundle (the persisted alias is checked, never used for selection). Callee validation runs after the parent call visit/start publication and after authored-input, managed/runtime-input, write-root, checksum and applicable resume-bound-input validation, and before the callee frame or state is created or changed and before any callee effect. Nested scopes apply the rule recursively.
+- Audit entry points are the module-level name `audit_scope` in `orchestrator.workflow.calls`, `orchestrator.workflow.executor` and `orchestrator.cli.commands.resume`.
+
+### Explicit identities
+Every present explicit durable step identity (`steps.<name>.step_id`, `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`) resolves to exactly one candidate in its owning scope. Qualified loop and call identities resolve only through projection-owned candidates: no splitting, normalizing, prefix matching, reconstruction, remapping or backfill, and no selection by workflow, module or procedure name, basename, path suffix or persisted alias. An absent `steps.*.step_id` stays compatible for every schema-valid row the presentation/name/order fallback supports, whatever its status, and stays absent. `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.*.call_step_id`, frame current position and loop progress selectors are mandatory.
+
+### Diagnostic
+`{"type": "resume_projection_integrity_error", "message": "Resume projection integrity audit failed: <reason>", "context": {...}}`. All eight context keys listed here are always present (the task prose says "seven" but lists eight): `diagnostic_schema` = `resume_projection_integrity_error.v1`; `reason`; `scope_path` (`{"kind": "root", "workflow_file": <file>}` then one `{"kind": "call_frame", "frame_id": <id>}` per frame entered); `field` (dotted path or null); `offending_value` (scalar, or null when absent or a container); `expected_owner` = `{workflow_file, workflow_checksum, projection_scope}` with `projection_scope` `root` or `call_frame`; `candidate_count` (integer or null); `call_boundary_step_id` (frame call step id or null).
+
+| reason | field | offending_value | candidate_count |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `unknown_explicit_step_id` | `steps.<name>.step_id` | the id | 0 |
+| `presentation_slot_mismatch` | `steps.<name>.step_id` or `current_step.step_id` | the id | 1 |
+| `out_of_scope_step_id` | `current_step.step_id` or `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id` | the id | 0 |
+| `unclaimed_explicit_step_row` | `steps.<name>.step_id` | the id | 0 |
+| `missing_required_identity` | e.g. `current_step.step_id` | null | null |
+| `missing_call_boundary` | `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id` | the id | 0 (`call_boundary_step_id` = id) |
+| `ambiguous_call_boundary` | `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id` | the id | the count |
+| `ambiguous_resumable_call_frame` | `call_frames` | number of resumable frames | that number |
+| `persisted_import_alias_mismatch` | `call_frames.<id>.import_alias` | persisted alias | 1 |
+| `missing_imported_bundle` | `imports.<alias>` | the alias | 1 |
+| `unsupported_shape` | `steps`, `call_frames`, `call_frames.<id>`, or the malformed identity | null for containers | null |
+| `invalid_loop_progress` | e.g. `repeat_until.<loop>.completed_iterations` | per field | null |
+
+Privacy: diagnostics never expose bound inputs, context values, artifacts, prompts, provider output, secrets, whole frame state, whole projections, or the contents of a malformed list or mapping used as an identity.
+
+### Loop projection integrity
+`for_each`: indices are unique non-boolean non-negative integers within `items`; optional `current_index` is in range and not completed. `repeat_until` admits four forms: (1) active: non-negative `current_iteration` not in `completed_iterations`, consistent condition/result fields; (2) terminal condition success: `current_iteration: null`, `last_condition_result: true`, not exhausted; (3) terminal successful exhaustion: covers the declared maximum range, `last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted: true`, completed loop step with completed `on_exhausted` outputs; (4) terminal failed exhaustion: covers the maximum range, `last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted` absent or false, failed loop step with `error.type` `repeat_until_iterations_exhausted`. Terminal forms keep completed-iteration history and identify the terminal evaluated iteration. Valid bookkeeping does not excuse stale loop-local ids or loop-contained call-boundary ids.
+
+`ResumeProjectionValidationError(reason, message)` pairs (for `for_each` `ProcessItems`, `repeat_until` `ReviewLoop`):
+- `unsupported_shape`: `for_each must be a mapping`; `for_each.ProcessItems must be a mapping`; `for_each.ProcessItems.items must be a list`; `for_each.ProcessItems.current_index must be null or a non-boolean integer`; `repeat_until must be a mapping`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop must be a mapping`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.current_iteration must be null or a non-boolean integer`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.exhausted must be boolean when present`.
+- `invalid_loop_progress`: `for_each.ProcessItems.completed_indices must not contain duplicate indices`; `for_each.ProcessItems.completed_indices contains an out-of-range index`; `for_each.ProcessItems.current_index is already completed`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.completed_iterations must not contain duplicate indices`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.current_iteration is out of range`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.completed_iterations is not valid prior history`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.condition result conflicts with evaluation state`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.terminal progress requires completed evaluated history`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.successful exhaustion requires a completed frame`; `repeat_until.ReviewLoop.failed exhaustion requires the terminal exhaustion error`.
+
+### Call frames and retry lineage
+Every frame validates caller identity, import alias, boundary ownership and status. Completed frames are unlimited historical records and never resumable. A boundary whose callee is not Workflow Lisp (`provenance.frontend_kind != "workflow_lisp"`; never a `.orc` suffix or a name) has at most one non-completed frame per frame family. A Workflow Lisp boundary's non-completed frames form a retry lineage: base `B` (ordinal 0) and `B::retry::<n>` (n = 1, 2, ...), zero or one running member and any number of failed predecessors. Mixed bases, several running members, a duplicate or missing ordinal, a malformed or nested own retry marker, an unknown status, or a caller or alias differing from the boundary's fail closed; mapping order never selects. The next fresh retry id is the lowest unused positive ordinal over all members, completed included.
+
+Frame families: a family is the frame id with its own trailing retry suffix removed, grouped within one resolved boundary. Only the text after the last `::visit::` is parsed (`<n>` or `<n>::retry::<k>`, ASCII digits, no leading zero, k >= 1); text before it, which may hold an inherited parent `::retry::`, is never inspected. Frames whose id has no valid own suffix share one family. At most one running member exists per boundary per parent scope. A family is *open* while it has no completed member and has a running or failed member; settled families are validated historical records. Re-entering a call step on resume bumps its visit counter, so a reached call continues the one open family (found by lineage, never by recomputing its id); several open families fail closed unless exactly one holds a running member.
+
+With a running member: its checksum and resume-bound-input validation run first and win on failure; then each failed predecessor is checksum-validated and recursively audited in ordinal order; then the running member's scope is audited and resumed. Without one: every failed predecessor is validated and audited before the fresh retry frame is allocated. A completed frame whose id collides with the frame a resumed call would occupy is rejected before lineage grouping, whatever its `call_step_id`, with `call_resume_state_invalid` whose context is exactly `{"step", "call", "call_frame_id", "reason": "call_resume_state_invalid", "detail": "completed_call_frame_id_collision"}`; no child manager or executor is created and frames are unchanged.
+
+### Checksum envelope
+A structurally root executor whose checksum recheck fails persists `error` = `{"type": "workflow_checksum_mismatch", "message": "Workflow has been modified since the run started", "context": {workflow_file, persisted_checksum, current_checksum, reason}}` (first three strings or null), `reason` one of `workflow_modified`, `missing_recorded_checksum`, `missing_workflow_path`, `workflow_unavailable`. The CLI root-checksum precheck keeps its existing plain behavior (exit 1, nothing written) before any session or executor exists and never writes this envelope.
+
+### Failure-envelope classes
+- ROOT class (CLI audit failure, executor checksum failure, executor root-audit failure): nothing has executed; the parsed `state.json` equals the original except `status` (`failed`), `error` and `updated_at`, with key order and unknown root fields preserved. Already-open session/process metadata may remain; a failing session close cannot replace the diagnostic. Byte identity is required only where nothing is written. The executor stops before the prologue and closes its session as failed.
+- REACHED-CALL class (nested or call-frame audit failure after the parent's ordinary visit/start publication): allowed writes are the caller's ordinary visit/start/loop-progress publication, the caller's failed call-step result and current/visit snapshot, each ancestor frame's ordinary failed snapshot, the root status/error, and configured finalization. The selected callee frame and its state directory stay unchanged and no callee effect runs. The root audit does not recurse into frame state. The diagnostic reaches the root unchanged, never wrapped in `call_failed`.
+
+### Sticky failure and finalization
+`resume_projection_integrity_error` is a sticky, non-routable terminal failure: authored `success`/`failure`/`always` routes, `on_error=continue` and loop continuation cannot consume it (`_handle_control_flow` returns `_stop`). Configured finalization may still run; a finalization failure is supplemental: `finalization.status: failed`, `finalization.failure` = `{"step": <cleanup step name>, "step_id": <its id>, "error": <the cleanup's projection error or null>}`, and it never wraps, replaces, clears or downgrades the run diagnostic. A projection error that arises only inside finalization becomes the run error and `finalization.failure.error`. Epilogue and session close preserve it.
+
+### Forensic current_step
+An unchanged `current_step` left by an envelope of type `resume_projection_integrity_error` or `workflow_checksum_mismatch` is forensic: the run is reported failed with its error; `build_status_snapshot` shows that step `pending` (status and summary `pending`, `current_visit_count` null, counted under pending), `render_status_markdown` does not say `in progress`, and heartbeat/stalled-run interpretation does not treat it as live.
+
+### YAML import diagnostics
+Classic-YAML loading rejects, before bundle construction, an alias occurring more than once among effective `imports` entries (duplicate keys, `<<` merge in `imports`, repeated top-level `imports`, or top-level `<<` sources each carrying `imports`) as `imports.<alias>: duplicate import alias` naming each `line L, column C` (1-based, alias key), reported once. Repeated top-level `imports` sections with distinct aliases are reported once as `imports: duplicate top-level mapping` naming each `imports` key position, not as a duplicate alias. A single merged `imports` with unique aliases loads.
+
+### Typed Workflow Lisp provenance
+Every bundle from the Workflow Lisp compiler (validated, lowered, imported Workflow Lisp) carries `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`. A YAML bundle passed through `imported_workflow_bundles` keeps its provenance and is linked as the same object. `build_frontend_bundle` keeps the compiler-set value.
+
+### Non-goals
+No receipts, persisted audit caches, procedure-migration or identity-retirement evidence, or migrations. State schema stays `2.1`; pre-v2.0 and pre-2.1 reusable-call state remain rejected.
